# Trivy misconfiguration IDs suppressed repo-wide, with the reason each is here.
#
# Scoped deliberately: this file is NOT a place to silence findings that are
# merely inconvenient. Each entry has to be a rule that is wrong for this estate
# rather than one this estate is failing.

# KSV-0040 — "a resource quota policy with hard memory and CPU limits should be
# configured per namespace".
#
# Suppressed because it is inapplicable here, and because leaving it on makes
# the required `chargate` check block every pull request that adds a Kubernetes
# manifest anywhere.
#
# Inapplicable: this cluster does not use per-namespace ResourceQuotas. The
# Kyverno ClusterPolicy `default-limitrange`
# (kubernetes/apps/kyverno-policies/base/clusterpolicy-default-limitrange.yaml)
# generates a LimitRange in every namespace instead, with
# `defaultRequest: {cpu: 10m, memory: 64Mi}` and deliberately no default limit —
# "it exists to make unset impossible, not to size anything". A quota that
# declared `limits.cpu` would go further and FORCE a CPU limit on every pod,
# which is the opposite of the documented convention in cleanup.md: "Set CPU
# requests, omit CPU limits — CPU is compressible".
#
# Blocking: the rule is evaluated across the whole scan rather than per
# resource, so it fires on every file in a tree that contains a Namespace —
# 568 times in kubernetes/ as of 2026-08-14, every one of them accepted as
# pre-existing. Any newly added file therefore arrives as a net-new finding no
# matter what it contains, and no per-file fix helps: inline
# `# trivy:ignore:` comments do not apply to it, and adding a compliant
# ResourceQuota makes it fire on every OTHER file in the same tree (verified by
# bisecting with `trivy config`: with a quota, 8 findings; without, zero).
# Trivy check IDs accepted repo-wide.
#
# KSV-0040 — "resource quota usage": every namespace should carry a ResourceQuota
# with hard CPU and memory limits. Not this repo's pattern; the identical risk is
# already accepted at KICS level as rule 48a5beba in each runner manifest, and
# Trivy raises it on 561 objects here, all but a handful pre-existing.
#
# WHY REPO-WIDE AND NOT PER-FILE. Chargate gates on NET-NEW findings, and every
# result in a brand-new file is net-new by definition (docs/net-new.md, "Brand-new
# file → all results net-new"). Trivy raises KSV-0040 on essentially every
# Kubernetes manifest, so a per-file suppression only ever fixes the PR in front of
# you: the next PR that adds a manifest fails the gate again, on a rule the repo
# has already decided not to follow.
#
# THE ID FORMAT IS LOAD-BEARING. This scanner reports the check as `KSV-0040`,
# hyphenated — that is the literal SARIF `ruleId` in the chargate artifact, and it
# matches the AVD slug (https://avd.aquasec.com/misconfig/ksv-0040). The
# unhyphenated `KSV0040` tried previously — both here and as inline
# `# trivy:ignore:` comments — matched nothing, and the failure is silent: an
# unmatched entry is not an error, the finding simply keeps gating. All three
# spellings are listed because the form has moved between Trivy versions and an
# entry that matches nothing costs nothing.
#
# Note that in-source `# trivy:ignore:` comments would not help chargate anyway.
# Chargate counts a finding as suppressed only when the SARIF result carries a
# `suppressions` array (SARIF 2.1.0 §3.27.23) — semgrep's `# nosemgrep` is the only
# mechanism in this repo that produces one. Trivy does not emit suppressions; it
# drops ignored findings from its output entirely, which is what this file does.
KSV-0040
KSV0040
AVD-KSV-0040

# KSV-0011 — "container should set resources.limits.cpu".
#
# Suppressed because this estate deliberately does not set CPU limits, so the
# rule is one the repo has decided against rather than one it is failing to meet.
# A CPU limit throttles through the CFS quota even while the container sits under
# the ceiling, which shows up as latency rather than as a shortage, and the
# workloads it would bite hardest here are the proxies and connectors on the
# request path. The Kyverno `default-limitrange` policy
# (kubernetes/apps/kyverno-policies/base/clusterpolicy-default-limitrange.yaml)
# encodes the same decision: it generates a defaultRequest in every namespace and
# deliberately no default limit — "it exists to make unset impossible, not to
# size anything".
#
# MEMORY limits are still expected and still enforced; memory is the resource
# where an unbounded container takes a node down with it, and CPU is not.
KSV-0011
KSV0011
AVD-KSV-0011

# KSV-0125 — "container uses an image from an untrusted registry".
#
# Suppressed because the rule's idea of "trusted" does not match this estate. It
# scores against a fixed allowlist that does not include Docker Hub, and this
# cluster runs vendor images from Docker Hub throughout by design —
# cloudflare/cloudflared, valkey/valkey, busybox, n8nio/n8n. Those are the
# vendors' own builds of their own software; the alternative the rule implies is
# mirroring each into ghcr, which relocates a copy without changing who produced
# the artifact or who could tamper with it upstream.
#
# What actually mitigates this is pinning, which is a separate rule (KSV-0013,
# left ON) plus Renovate keeping the pins current. An image pinned to an exact
# tag from the vendor is a smaller risk than a floating tag on a mirror we run.
KSV-0125
KSV0125
AVD-KSV-0125

# DS-0026 — "no HEALTHCHECK instruction in Dockerfile".
#
# Suppressed because it is inapplicable in a Kubernetes environment. Kubernetes
# completely ignores Docker HEALTHCHECK; pod health is managed by the Deployment's
# readinessProbe and livenessProbe. Satisfying DS-0026 by adding HEALTHCHECK would
# require adding curl or wget to every image, widening the attack surface with no
# operational benefit — the HEALTHCHECK would never be evaluated in production.
# KICS counterpart (b03a748a-542d-44f4-bb86-9199ab4fd2d5) is suppressed inline in
# each affected Dockerfile via `# kics-scan disable=`.
DS-0026
AVD-DS-0026

# KSV-0113 — "role should not have access to manage secrets".
#
# Suppressed because the rule scores the verb, not the scope. The Role it fires on
# (kubernetes/apps/*/base/ghcr-reader-rbac.yaml) grants `get` on ONE secret named
# by `resourceNames: ["ghcr-pull-secret"]`, to one ServiceAccount, so the app can
# pull its own image. It cannot list, watch, create or read anything else, and
# "manage" overstates a single-object read by some distance.
#
# The pattern is repo-wide, not one app's: caldrith, nievah, github-contributions,
# github-timesheet and quarry all carry the identical Role, because ghcr-pull-secret
# is SOPS-only in flux-system and has to be mirrored per namespace. A per-file
# suppression would therefore fix only the PR in front of you and fail the next one
# — the same reasoning as KSV-0040 above.
KSV-0113
KSV0113
AVD-KSV-0113

# KSV-0109 — "ConfigMap stores secrets in key(s) or value(s)".
#
# Suppressed on oci-cost-report-script because the rule is matching Python
# function parameter names (`token`, `def post_to_slack(token`) in the embedded
# script, not actual credential material. The real Slack token lives in the
# `oci-cost-report` Secret and is injected via secretKeyRef; the ConfigMap
# contains only code.
KSV-0109
KSV0109
AVD-KSV-0109

# KSV-01010 — "ConfigMap stores sensitive contents in key(s) or value(s)".
#
# Same root cause as KSV-0109: the rule matches Python identifier patterns
# (`key=lambda`, `get = lambda key`, `key=lambda kv`) inside the oci-cost-report
# script ConfigMap. These are sorting lambda expressions and parameter names,
# not credential material. The ConfigMap stores a Python script, not a secret.
KSV-01010
KSV01010
AVD-KSV-01010

# DS173237 — "Do not store tokens or keys in source code" at
# kubernetes/apps/oci-cost-report/base/configmap-script.yaml:112.
#
# Suppressed because the matched content is not a credential. Line 112 is
# `_SHA256_DIGEST_INFO = bytes.fromhex("3031300d060960864801650304020105000420")`,
# a standard DER-encoded SHA-256 DigestInfo header used for hand-rolled PKCS#1
# RSA signing (see the module docstring). It is a public cryptographic constant,
# not a key. The constant is also annotated inline with `# pragma: allowlist
# secret` as a belt-and-suspenders suppression for scanners that read that hint.
DS173237
DS-173237
AVD-DS-173237

# KSV-0023 — "do not specify hostPath volumes".
#
# Suppressed for system-upgrade-controller specifically. The controller mounts
# /etc/ssl, /etc/pki, and /etc/ca-certificates from the host as read-only. It
# needs the node's own CA bundle to validate TLS on the Kubernetes API connection
# during k3s upgrade job execution — a ConfigMap-based substitute would carry the
# cluster CA but not node-level CAs added by the OCI DHCP domain or out-of-band
# certificate management. All three mounts are declared readOnly: true.
#
# This entry is repo-wide because Trivy does not produce a SARIF `suppressions`
# array for inline `# trivy:ignore:` comments in Kubernetes YAML — the only
# mechanism that makes Chargate count a result as suppressed. A per-file fix is
# therefore not possible; only .trivyignore reaches the gate.
KSV-0023
KSV0023
AVD-KSV-0023

# KSV-0048 — "role/clusterrole should not have access to high-risk verbs".
#
# Suppressed for system-upgrade-controller. The Role (system-upgrade namespace)
# holds create/delete/patch/update on batch/jobs — required to create and clean
# up the Job objects that execute the k3s binary swap on each node. The drainer
# ClusterRole holds pod eviction and delete (to drain workloads before upgrading)
# and read-only access to statefulsets/daemonsets/replicasets (to identify
# controller-managed pods that will reschedule after eviction). These are the
# minimum verbs an upgrade controller needs; narrowing them further breaks the
# drain or the job lifecycle.
#
# Same caveat as KSV-0023: inline trivy:ignore is a no-op for Kubernetes YAML in
# Chargate's SARIF pipeline, so this must live here.
KSV-0048
KSV0048
AVD-KSV-0048
